WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.749
<v Examiner>The eye, part by part.

2
00:00:02.749 --> 00:00:05.122
<v Examiner>Her worksheets and her matching tables.

3
00:00:05.572 --> 00:00:09.111
Her eye labelling and matching questions carry a lot of marks:

4
00:00:09.111 --> 00:00:13.792
one matching table alone is twenty marks, and her worksheets add about fifty more.

5
00:00:13.792 --> 00:00:16.475
The three layers track gives you the structure.

6
00:00:16.475 --> 00:00:19.329
This one walks through the eye from front to back,

7
00:00:19.329 --> 00:00:24.409
the way her worksheet describes each part, so you can name any part from its description.

8
00:00:24.859 --> 00:00:30.363
The hook, front to back: conjunctiva, cornea, aqueous, iris and pupil, lens, vitreous,

9
00:00:30.363 --> 00:00:33.947
retina, choroid, sclera, and out through the blind spot.

10
00:00:34.397 --> 00:00:35.016
The front.

11
00:00:35.016 --> 00:00:40.341
The conjunctiva is the thin mucous membrane that covers the front of the eye and lines

12
00:00:40.341 --> 00:00:41.950
the inside of the eyelids.

13
00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:47.027
It's a protective layer of epithelial cells, guarding the cornea against friction,

14
00:00:47.027 --> 00:00:50.061
and tears from the lacrimal glands keep it moist.

15
00:00:50.061 --> 00:00:52.042
It's what's infected in pinkeye.

16
00:00:52.042 --> 00:00:56.994
The cornea is the clear, curved window at the front, continuous with the sclera.

17
00:00:56.994 --> 00:01:01.823
Light is first bent here, and it does about eighty per cent of the refraction.

18
00:01:01.823 --> 00:01:05.105
Her worksheet adds that a cornea can be transplanted.

19
00:01:05.555 --> 00:01:10.345
Behind the cornea is the anterior chamber, holding the aqueous humour, a clear,

20
00:01:10.345 --> 00:01:13.619
watery fluid that keeps the front of the eye in shape.

21
00:01:13.619 --> 00:01:19.016
It's drained by the canal of Schlemm, a circular canal where the sclera meets the cornea.

22
00:01:19.466 --> 00:01:23.195
The iris is the coloured part: a pigmented muscular ring,

23
00:01:23.195 --> 00:01:28.101
with an inner ring of circular muscle, and an outer layer of radial muscle.

24
00:01:28.101 --> 00:01:32.027
It controls how much light gets in: enough to form an image,

25
00:01:32.027 --> 00:01:34.840
but not so much that it damages the retina.

26
00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:40.596
Her key on eye colour: people with blue eyes make lesser amounts of brown pigment in the

27
00:01:40.596 --> 00:01:40.924
iris.

28
00:01:40.924 --> 00:01:44.064
The pupil is the hole in the middle of the iris.

29
00:01:44.064 --> 00:01:49.363
It constricts in bright light, and dilates in dim light, under autonomic control.

30
00:01:50.813 --> 00:01:51.494
The middle.

31
00:01:51.494 --> 00:01:55.893
The ciliary body holds the lens in place with its suspensory ligaments,

32
00:01:55.893 --> 00:02:01.160
secretes the aqueous humour, and contains the ciliary muscles, which are involuntary,

33
00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:04.258
and change the shape of the lens for near and far.

34
00:02:04.258 --> 00:02:05.559
That's accommodation.

35
00:02:05.559 --> 00:02:08.905
The lens itself is transparent, flexible and biconvex,

36
00:02:08.905 --> 00:02:12.189
and does the other twenty per cent of the refraction.

37
00:02:12.639 --> 00:02:18.000
Behind the lens is the vitreous humour: a transparent, jelly-like, protein-rich mass.

38
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.343
It maintains the shape of the eyeball, the back part,

39
00:02:21.343 --> 00:02:24.182
and her worksheet says it provides nutrients.

40
00:02:24.182 --> 00:02:28.975
A small, transparent canal runs through it, from the optic disc to the lens:

41
00:02:28.975 --> 00:02:30.111
the hyaloid canal.

42
00:02:31.561 --> 00:02:32.162
The back.

43
00:02:32.162 --> 00:02:34.299
The retina is the sensory layer,

44
00:02:34.299 --> 00:02:38.907
and her key says the sensory layer is the one responsible for vision.

45
00:02:38.907 --> 00:02:44.317
Its rods and cones turn light into electrical impulses, and the image forms here.

46
00:02:44.317 --> 00:02:47.322
Cones for colour vision, rods for poor light.

47
00:02:47.322 --> 00:02:50.995
At its centre is the macula lutea, an oval yellow spot.

48
00:02:50.995 --> 00:02:57.006
In the middle of that is the fovea centralis, a small pit directly in line with the pupil,

49
00:02:57.006 --> 00:03:00.412
with only cones: sharp colour vision in good light.

50
00:03:00.862 --> 00:03:06.037
The optic disc, the blind spot, is the exit point for the ganglion cell axons,

51
00:03:06.037 --> 00:03:08.426
where they leave as the optic nerve.

52
00:03:08.426 --> 00:03:11.677
It has no rods or cones, so no image forms there.

53
00:03:11.677 --> 00:03:17.118
The optic nerve carries the impulses to the occipital lobe, which controls vision.

54
00:03:17.568 --> 00:03:21.484
The choroid is the black layer between the retina and the sclera.

55
00:03:21.484 --> 00:03:25.460
Its dark pigment stops light reflecting around inside the eyeball,

56
00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:29.556
and its network of blood vessels feeds the retina and removes waste.

57
00:03:29.556 --> 00:03:33.110
And the sclera is the tough, white, non-elastic outer coat.

58
00:03:33.110 --> 00:03:37.568
It protects the eye, keeps it round with the pressure of the fluid inside,

59
00:03:37.568 --> 00:03:39.797
and anchors the muscles that move it.

60
00:03:41.247 --> 00:03:42.750
Her one-mark eye facts.

61
00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:47.650
Of all the senses, vision has the majority of the body's sensory receptors.

62
00:03:47.650 --> 00:03:52.027
Strabismus is when a person can't align both eyes at the same time.

63
00:03:52.027 --> 00:03:57.776
Retinal detachment is when the retina tears and comes away, and it's treated with laser.

64
00:03:57.776 --> 00:04:03.263
Myopia, in her tables: the lens is too strong, or too bulgy, or the eye is too long,

65
00:04:03.263 --> 00:04:06.726
so the light is over-focused, in front of the retina,

66
00:04:06.726 --> 00:04:09.208
and a biconcave lens scatters it back.

67
00:04:09.208 --> 00:04:13.782
Hyperopia: the lens is too weak, or too flat, or the eye is too short,

68
00:04:13.782 --> 00:04:18.355
so the light focuses behind the retina, and a biconvex lens boosts it.

69
00:04:18.805 --> 00:04:21.024
Two words from her short questions.

70
00:04:21.024 --> 00:04:25.208
An afterimage is an image that persists after you close your eyes.

71
00:04:25.208 --> 00:04:30.533
Her key says it's thought to be due to a vivid stimulus blocking the photoreceptors,

72
00:04:30.533 --> 00:04:33.005
so they can't fire again straight away.

73
00:04:33.005 --> 00:04:36.429
And an inverted image has two meanings in her quizzes.

74
00:04:36.429 --> 00:04:41.627
In her lens drop-down, inverted means upside down: the biconvex lens makes a real,

75
00:04:41.627 --> 00:04:44.607
inverted image, and the brain turns it upright.

76
00:04:44.607 --> 00:04:49.552
In her short-answer quiz, her model answer means an image in opposite colours:

77
00:04:49.552 --> 00:04:53.736
stare at it, look away, and the afterimage shows the true colours.

78
00:04:54.186 --> 00:04:54.842
The traps.

79
00:04:54.842 --> 00:04:59.766
The aqueous humour is at the front, the vitreous humour is behind the lens.

80
00:04:59.766 --> 00:05:04.033
The optic disc is the blind spot; the fovea is the sharpest spot.

81
00:05:04.033 --> 00:05:09.482
And the choroid is black on purpose: it stops light bouncing around inside the eye.

82
00:05:09.932 --> 00:05:10.525
<v Examiner>Once more.

83
00:05:10.525 --> 00:05:11.831
<v Examiner>The eye, part by part.

84
00:05:14.281 --> 00:05:18.426
The conjunctiva covers the front of the eye and lines the eyelids,

85
00:05:18.426 --> 00:05:20.248
and it's infected in pinkeye.

86
00:05:20.248 --> 00:05:23.829
The cornea is the clear window where light is first bent:

87
00:05:23.829 --> 00:05:27.723
eighty per cent of the refraction, and it can be transplanted.

88
00:05:27.723 --> 00:05:33.125
The aqueous humour fills the anterior chamber and drains through the canal of Schlemm.

89
00:05:33.125 --> 00:05:38.088
The iris has circular and radial muscle, and blue eyes have less brown pigment.

90
00:05:38.088 --> 00:05:41.480
The ciliary body holds the lens, makes aqueous humour,

91
00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:44.621
and its involuntary muscles change the lens shape.

92
00:05:44.621 --> 00:05:48.138
The lens is biconvex: twenty per cent of the refraction.

93
00:05:48.138 --> 00:05:53.415
The vitreous humour is the jelly behind the lens, with the hyaloid canal through it.

94
00:05:53.865 --> 00:05:57.700
The retina is the sensory layer, where the image forms.

95
00:05:57.700 --> 00:06:02.929
The macula lutea is the yellow spot, and the fovea, in line with the pupil,

96
00:06:02.929 --> 00:06:03.975
has only cones.

97
00:06:03.975 --> 00:06:09.275
The optic disc, the blind spot, is where the axons leave as the optic nerve,

98
00:06:09.275 --> 00:06:10.809
to the occipital lobe.

99
00:06:10.809 --> 00:06:15.202
The choroid is black, to stop reflection, and feeds the retina.

100
00:06:15.202 --> 00:06:19.037
The sclera is tough and white, and keeps the eye round.

101
00:06:19.037 --> 00:06:21.826
Vision has most of the body's receptors.

102
00:06:21.826 --> 00:06:24.127
Strabismus: the eyes can't align.

103
00:06:24.127 --> 00:06:26.916
Myopia: too bulgy a lens, biconcave fix.

104
00:06:26.916 --> 00:06:29.287
Hyperopia: too flat, biconvex fix.
